# 📦 File Condenser — Project Concept & TODO PROJECT CONDENSER IS okay..  But we are naming it !!!BLACKHOLE!!!

## 🔥 Motivation

University project submission systems often place strict file count limits (e.g., max 100 non-binary files), mainly to prevent students from submitting unnecessary build artifacts or junk directories.

However, these constraints inadvertently punish large, modular, well-structured codebases — especially when a student (me) goes **way beyond the assignment scope**.

Instead of asking for a file count exception like a normal person, I chose to build a tool that collapses an **entire project directory (with hundreds of source and test files)** into a **single executable Python (or C++) script** that can fully **reconstruct the original file structure and contents** upon execution.

Yes, it’s petty.
Yes, it’s overkill.
Yes, I’m 100% doing it.

---

## 🧠 Concept

> **File Condenser** takes any project directory and generates a *single file* (e.g., `extract_all.py`) that:
> - Encodes every file (text, possibly binary) and directory structure
> - Outputs a complete self-extracting script
> - When run, it reconstructs the full project tree exactly as it was

---

## ✨ Features (Planned)

- [ ] **Support nested directories**
- [ ] **Accurate file recreation with relative paths**
- [ ] **Readable, auto-generated Python (or C++) output**
- [ ] **Optional theatrical mode**: each directory becomes a class, each file a method
- [ ] **Reconstruction progress logs**
- [ ] **Dry-run / preview mode**
- [ ] **Optional compression for large files**
- [ ] **Blacklist support**: ignore `.git`, `build/`, etc.
- [ ] **Post-extraction hook** (e.g., run `make`, install deps)
- [ ] **Command-line tool interface** (e.g., `python condenser.py project/ output.py`)

---

## 🤹 Example Use Case

### Input:
    A well-structured project:

        my_project/
        ├── src/
        │ ├── main.cpp
        │ └── lexer.cpp
        ├── include/
        │ └── token.hpp
        └── tests/
        └── test_expr.cpp

### Output:
    extract_all.py



### Running `extract_all.py` will:
- Recreate the full `my_project/` directory
- Recreate all file contents
- Print status log: `"✔️ Reconstructed src/lexer.cpp"`

---

## 📍 File Condenser Modes

| Mode            | Description                                       |
|-----------------|---------------------------------------------------|
| `--theatrical`  | Class per dir, method per file (performance art)  |
| `--dry-run`     | Just show what would be extracted                 |
| `--output`      | Set output file name (default: `extract_all.py`)  |

